ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".

**STRIDE SCHEDULER IMPLEMENTATION**

Lwazi Mkhwanazi
Moussa Seye

**Overview**:
This project implements a stride scheduler in xv6.
A new system call (setStride) and a modification to the scheduler
ensure proportional CPU sharing based on process stride values. 
Lower stride -> more CPU time

**FILES MODIFIED**

**Kernel**

* kernel/proc.h
    * added int stride;
    * added int pass;
    * added to struct proc.

* kernel/proc.c
    * updated allocproc() to initialize stride and pass.
    * Modified scheduler() to select process with the lowest pass value.
    * Added global passTotal to track the total of passes.
    * Added logic to increment pass by stride.

* kernel/sysproc.c
    * Added implementation of sys_setStride()

* kernel/syscall.c
    * Added syscall entry for SYS_setStride.

* kernel/syscall.h
    * Added syscall number for SYS_setStride.

**User**

* user.h
    * Added prototype: int setStride(int);

* usys.pl 
    * Added entry("setStride");

* getpinfo.c
    * Added code to print PID, INUSE, RUNTIME & PASS.

**How the scheduler works**

    Each process has:
        * stride - determines the share of CPU
        * pass - scheduler metric that increases after each run
        * Lower pass is selected to run next

    Scheduling Algorithm:
        1. Find all runnable processes
        2. Choose the process with the minimum pass value
        3. Run it and update: p->pass += p->stride;
    3
    System Call setStride()
        * Used by user processes to assign their CPU share.
        Valid stride >= 1.

**Testing and Evidence**

    We created multiple long running processes with different strides and ran getpinfo to see how the processes were behaving.

